WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.700 --> 00:00:06.190
It read a quarter of this photo's pixels, and found 3 critical findings.

2
00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:08.793
It will not sign them off. A person must.

3
00:00:09.543 --> 00:00:16.450
Drishyam, an Agentic Vision entry to the OpenCV AI Competition, powered by AWS.

4
00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:23.075
Built by Vaibhav Jangid, lead engineer, and Bhavya Dhoot, design and presentation.

5
00:00:23.825 --> 00:00:26.434
America has about 625,000 bridges,

6
00:00:26.444 --> 00:00:30.931
each due a routine inspection at least every two years.

7
00:00:30.981 --> 00:00:32.247
In an Oregon study,

8
00:00:32.257 --> 00:00:37.544
the largest drone inspection produced more than 2,500 photos of one bridge.

9
00:00:37.594 --> 00:00:40.230
Drishyam is for the engineer reviewing them:

10
00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:44.459
it zooms in where needed, and hands critical findings to a person.

11
00:00:45.159 --> 00:00:50.386
The live site runs on AWS: OpenCV 5, on an arm Lambda.

12
00:00:50.436 --> 00:00:55.349
Watch it inspect a bridge. A privacy pass runs first; nothing to blur here.

13
00:00:55.399 --> 00:00:58.842
One survey look at the whole photo, at one eighth size.

14
00:00:58.892 --> 00:01:03.797
Entropy 0.69 is above 0.5. Unsure, so it zooms in.

15
00:01:03.847 --> 00:01:07.761
Then it moves to the best region, and double-checks at full resolution.

16
00:01:07.811 --> 00:01:11.391
At full resolution the masks disagree: unconfirmed.

17
00:01:11.401 --> 00:01:14.326
Exposed rebar always goes to a person.

18
00:01:14.376 --> 00:01:18.674
Then a second spall. It stops when the next look costs more than it is worth,

19
00:01:18.684 --> 00:01:21.956
and so it misses a crack in a region it never visited.

20
00:01:22.006 --> 00:01:25.571
The agent took under a second; the page replays it slowly.

21
00:01:25.621 --> 00:01:30.619
Only a person can approve. A name, Approve, and the server signs the work order.

22
00:01:30.669 --> 00:01:34.626
Change one character of that signature, and the server refuses it.

23
00:01:35.276 --> 00:01:36.438
Now, break it.

24
00:01:36.538 --> 00:01:40.456
Slow the segmenter down, and the governor switches to the fast model.

25
00:01:40.556 --> 00:01:42.821
The planner drill sends a shell command.

26
00:01:42.831 --> 00:01:46.265
The allow-list rejects it, and the rule planner decides.

27
00:01:46.365 --> 00:01:51.875
KILL halts the job before its next look; Resume replays the moves so far and carries on.

28
00:01:51.975 --> 00:01:56.569
No language model runs live. These drill planners are scripted to fail.

29
00:01:57.319 --> 00:02:02.439
Over MCP, Claude drove the same OpenCV tools on the same photo.

30
00:02:02.489 --> 00:02:07.590
At call 6, the full-resolution mask disagreed, and exposed rebar is critical:

31
00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:09.709
Claude handed it to a human.

32
00:02:09.759 --> 00:02:13.192
At call 18, the masks agreed: a spall, confirmed.

33
00:02:13.202 --> 00:02:16.263
At call 20, the budget ended the search.

34
00:02:16.313 --> 00:02:21.160
It still missed the large spall. The rules are in the prompt; the values were measured.

35
00:02:21.910 --> 00:02:26.540
Every step is perception, decision and action, with a human gate at the end.

36
00:02:26.590 --> 00:02:32.429
CloudFront and a rate-limiting firewall front a signed Function URL and the arm Lambda.

37
00:02:32.479 --> 00:02:36.851
DynamoDB holds the kill flags, Secrets Manager the signing key,

38
00:02:36.861 --> 00:02:38.920
and CloudWatch the alarms.

39
00:02:38.970 --> 00:02:42.029
OpenCV does the looking: a pyramid, saliency,

40
00:02:42.039 --> 00:02:47.882
warpPerspective as a virtual pan-tilt-zoom camera, a blur gate, and face blurring.

41
00:02:47.932 --> 00:02:50.843
Live, it uses the classical segmenter;

42
00:02:50.853 --> 00:02:55.330
OpenCV 5's new DNN engine is on the optional ONNX path.

43
00:02:55.380 --> 00:03:02.521
On Lambda, a step took under 90 ms at p95, over 3 smoke runs. COOL was not run.

44
00:03:03.221 --> 00:03:08.934
On 200 synthetic photos, it finds 87 % of exhaustive tiling's recall,

45
00:03:08.944 --> 00:03:15.331
at 26 % of the pixels: short of the 90 % target, though not significantly.

46
00:03:15.381 --> 00:03:21.891
Against one downscaled pass it scores 0.96×: slightly behind, within the noise.

47
00:03:21.941 --> 00:03:26.850
On larger photos it beats one downscale, which reads a sixth as many pixels;

48
00:03:26.860 --> 00:03:29.958
quarter-size tiling does as well with fewer.

49
00:03:30.008 --> 00:03:33.598
Ablations: only dropping the survey look loses recall;

50
00:03:33.608 --> 00:03:38.858
the others lose none, and a random gaze order matches it on smaller photos.

51
00:03:38.908 --> 00:03:44.445
On 200 real CODEBRIM photos, every method finds about 1 defect in 5;

52
00:03:44.455 --> 00:03:47.479
its boxes at random, about 1 in 8.

53
00:03:47.529 --> 00:03:53.374
On 51 synthetic photos with no damage, it still reports something 2 times in 3.

54
00:03:53.424 --> 00:03:55.821
Results for a trained segmenter are pending.

55
00:03:56.521 --> 00:04:01.419
Failures, in the open: graffiti read as spalling, a formwork line as a crack,

56
00:04:01.429 --> 00:04:03.973
glare left for a re-shoot, and blur.

57
00:04:04.023 --> 00:04:05.939
2 of these rules are not built yet.

58
00:04:05.949 --> 00:04:10.545
The straight-line test is built, but it let this line through at a coarse look.

59
00:04:10.595 --> 00:04:14.708
On a real photo, exposed rebar was reported as corrosion.

60
00:04:15.408 --> 00:04:21.276
Pixels, not millimetres. AI-assisted, not an inspection. Only a person can approve.

61
00:04:23.026 --> 00:04:28.728
make test, make eval, and one script to deploy, test and destroy the AWS stack.

62
00:04:29.378 --> 00:04:33.042
Drishyam. It looks where it matters, and asks a human first.
